  "reason": "The records collectively supply several relevant ingredients: R2, R3, and R15 establish the FCC/B2 lamellar architecture in closely related Al-Co-Cr-Fe-Ni eutectic alloys; R4 and R7 describe combined high-pressure/high-temperature treatment of an HEA, while R16-R19 show that pressure can reorganize HEA crystal structures through defects and phase transformations. Most importantly, R6 directly teaches that coherent interfaces facilitate dislocation transmission and relieve crack-producing stress concentrations, and R8 shows coherent nanoscale features can form in a closely related Al-containing FCC/B2 HEA. Other records support the usefulness of stacking faults, twins, dislocations, and multiple deformation mechanisms. Nevertheless, none connects HPHT treatment of Al0.7CoCrFeNi to conversion of incoherent FCC/B2 lamellae into a coherent hierarchical pattern, nor predicts preferential FCC-to-B2 dislocation transfer. Selecting HPHT for that specific architectural transformation therefore still requires a substantial new idea, so the set points in the right direction but does not fully reconstruct the result."
